## 3. Operators as a grammar

```ebnf
operation ::= grow_root | grow_shoot | grow_leaf | grow_fruit
            | absorb | photosynthesize | checkpoint | inspect ;
grow_root ::= "grow-root" root_id ;
grow_shoot ::= "grow-shoot" shoot_id ;
grow_leaf ::= "grow-leaf" shoot_id "supported-by" root_id ;
grow_fruit ::= "grow-fruit" leaf_id "supported-by" root_id ;
```

This is an illustrative **surface contract**, not a parser of these exact strings. The Python model realizes analogous typed method calls. A future `.p9` grammar and bytecode backend must compile the operations without reducing the operational semantics to JSON data interpretation. JSON schemas are for interoperability and tests only.

{
  "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
  "$id": "urn:tre:core-contract:0.1",
  "title": "TRE/TREE typed machine execution contract",
  "description": "Interchange/audit projection only. Not native executable language syntax.",
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "required": ["protocol", "artifact_kind", "seed", "version", "content_id", "structure", "provenance"],
  "properties": {
    "protocol": {"const": "TRE/TREE/0.1"},
    "artifact_kind": {"enum": ["machine-state", "scene-ir", "physics-ir", "corpus", "compiled-fruit"]},
    "seed": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1},
    "version": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 0},
    "content_id": {"type": "string", "pattern": "^sha256:[a-f0-9]{64}$"},
    "structure": {
      "type": "object", "additionalProperties": false,
      "required": ["nodes", "couplings"],
      "properties": {
        "nodes": {"type": "array", "items": {"type":"object", "required":["id","kind","parent"], "properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"kind":{"type":"string"},"parent":{"type":["string","null"]}}}},
        "couplings": {"type": "array", "items": {"type":"object", "required":["provider","consumer","witness"], "properties":{"provider":{"type":"string"},"consumer":{"type":"string"},"witness":{"type":"string"}}}}
      }
    },
    "provenance": {"type": "object", "required": ["source", "license", "revision"], "properties": {"source":{"type":"string"}, "license":{"type":"string"}, "revision":{"type":"string"}}}
  }
}

## VIII. The crucial existing Timeless normalization risk

The original repository (`lib/engine/variants.ts`, `text.ts`) currently maps `أ إ آ ٱ` into `ا`, `ؤ` into `و`, `ئ` into `ي`, `ء` into `ا`, `ة` into `ه`, and `ى` into `ي`. The current engine is explicit that these are **projections** for studying 28 canonical source letters, but WAZNOMIA cannot assume that the originals remain uniquely reconstructible from that projection.

Proposed safe fix: *do not change the original source engine in this research phase*. Build a wrapper preserving:

```
RawSurface{utf8, unicode_nfc, codepoint_sequence, epoch, source_offset}
 -> OrthographicProjection{projection_id, version, loss_ledger}
 -> RasmState{glyphs, positional_slots, domain_products}
 -> PhonemicAnalysis{inventory, context, alternatives}
 -> MorphoLexicalAnalysis{root,pattern,lexeme,attestations}
 -> SemanticFunctionalAnalysis{evidence-graded function}
```

A historical manuscript's dots are *observed strokes or ink marks*; computational feature vectors are analyst-defined abstractions. Do not confuse the two.

